<!--
  athena-local-rules.xml — Wazuh manager-side suppressions for known
  noise patterns on Athena buoys.

  Lives at: /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml on the Wazuh manager
  (source instance `primary`). Wazuh loads local_rules.xml after the stock
  ruleset, so these overrides take effect on the next manager reload:

      sudo /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-control restart

  Each rule below documents:
    - The false-positive pattern that motivates the suppression
    - Operational evidence (which buoy hit it, when, why FP)
    - The narrow `<match>` / `<regex>` scope so legitimate alerts
      with the same parent rule still fire

  Adding a new suppression here MUST include this same context block —
  the goal is auditable suppressions, not silent rule mutation.
-->
<group name="rootcheck,athena-suppressions,ossec">

  <!--
    Rule 510 — rootcheck "Generic" signature false-positives on stock
    Debian system binaries.

    Why it FPs: the "Generic" rootcheck signature regex is
        'bash|^/bin/sh|file\.h|proc\.h|/dev/[^n]|^/bin/.*sh'
    This matches help-text + error-message string literals embedded
    in setuid-helper + coreutils-style binaries — they contain those
    substrings in their normal help/error messages.

    Iteration history:
      - 2026-05-16 v1: narrow path-list match
        (/usr/bin/diff|/usr/bin/sed|/usr/bin/awk|/usr/bin/grep|
         /usr/bin/cut|/usr/bin/sort|/usr/bin/tail|/usr/bin/head|
         /usr/bin/uniq|/usr/bin/tr). Verification: dpkg -V on each
        flagged file returned empty (clean package manifest).

      - 2026-05-18 v2 (this revision): widened to `(Generic).` match.
        A 2-day soak under v1 still produced rule-510 alerts on
        newly onboarded buoys because v1 missed two patterns:

          (a) Rootcheck reports paths WITHOUT the /usr prefix on
              merged-/usr Debian (/bin/diff, /bin/chfn, /bin/passwd
              etc.) — v1 only listed /usr/bin/* variants.

          (b) Additional FP-prone setuid binaries weren't in v1's
              list (/bin/chfn, /bin/chsh, /bin/passwd from the
              `passwd` package).

        Verification before widening (2026-05-18):
          - dpkg -V passwd diffutils → clean (only /etc/default/useradd
            shows a `c` config-edit flag, expected and irrelevant)
          - sha256sum across every fleet buoy for /usr/bin/{diff,
            passwd,chfn,chsh}: byte-identical, ruling out targeted
            tamper or fleet-wide compromise.

        Decision: stop maintaining the path-list (every new
        FP-prone binary we hadn't yet seen would require an edit).
        Match on the literal "(Generic)." signature-name suffix that
        rootcheck appends to every Generic-class detection. This
        suppresses ONLY the noisy Generic class. Named-signature
        rootkit detections (e.g., "(kbeast Rootkit).",
        "(tornkit Rootkit).") would NOT end in "(Generic)." and
        continue to alert normally.

    Tradeoff acknowledged: rootcheck's Generic signature is widely
    considered noisy enough that many Wazuh shops disable it outright
    or replace rootcheck with auditd/Falco for HIDS. v2 keeps
    rootcheck on but drops the noisiest class. Re-evaluate on next
    HIDS review.
  -->
  <rule id="100510" level="0">
    <if_sid>510</if_sid>
    <match>(Generic).</match>
    <description>Athena: suppress rootcheck Generic-signature FPs on stock Debian binaries (level=0). Named-signature 510 alerts still fire. See iteration history in athena-local-rules.xml.</description>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Rule 533 — "Listened ports status (netstat) changed" firing on
    tailscaled ephemeral UDP-port rotation.

    Why it FPs: tailscaled rotates source UDP ports for STUN /
    NAT-traversal — typical entries that come and go:
        udp 0.0.0.0:<random> <pid>/tailscaled
    The diff between netstat snapshots shows ONLY tailscaled lines
    changing; the security-relevant listening set (SSH, OpenCanary
    honeypots, Pi-hole, NextDNS) stays identical.

    Confirmed FP (2026-05-16): over 24h, every firing's diff was
    tailscaled-only.

    Action: DOWNGRADE to level=1 instead of full suppression (level=0)
    so a real port change on a non-tailscaled process still alerts
    at level=7 (parent rule 533 fires first and is suppressed only
    when this child rule matches). The level=1 archived events still
    flow through the buoy-forwarder into OCTO; they just don't
    trigger alert routing.

    Note: this is INTENTIONALLY less aggressive than the rootcheck
    rule above. The reason: an attacker could open a new listening
    port while tailscaled is ALSO rotating, and the log would still
    contain "tailscaled" so a naive <match> would silently drop the
    real alert. Downgrade-not-suppress preserves the archive trail.
  -->
  <rule id="100533" level="1">
    <if_sid>533</if_sid>
    <match>tailscaled</match>
    <description>Athena: downgrade rule 533 (netstat changed) when tailscaled in log — STUN port rotation noise. Still archived at level=1; check OCTO events.db for the full netstat diff if review needed.</description>
  </rule>

</group>
